---
title: GRØD
template: editorial
spreads:
  - heading: Notes, and transcripts
    body: >-
      Every meeting becomes one continuous, editable document. Summary,
      decisions, actions, sitting right beside anything I typed myself. The
      transcript is a click away if you want it.
    image: /shots/grod/briefing.webp
    alt: >-
      A meeting note in GRØD, headed 'Weekly product review', holding the story,
      the decisions and the risks, with a question about the meeting answered
      inline and stamped with the minute it was said.
  - heading: Remembers people, not speaker labels
    body: >-
      Voices persist across meetings, connected to real people, merged when
      needed, forgotten on purpose if I ask. Pick a few people and see every
      meeting they share.
    image: /shots/grod/people.webp
    alt: >-
      GRØD's People view, listing everyone due in today's meetings by name, with
      one attendee marked 'Not in People' and waiting to be matched.
  - heading: Search that actually makes sense
    body: >-
      Search covers notes, decisions, actions, people, and full transcripts,
      then jumps straight to the moment something was said. Ask a question
      across meetings and get an answer with a citation, not a guess.
    image: /shots/grod/search.webp
    alt: >-
      A search for 'confidence ribbon decision' in GRØD, returning the two
      people who discussed it and the two meetings where it was decided, the
      decision quoted in place.
  - heading: Calendar-aware, not calendar-first
    body: >-
      It knows what's on my calendar, so a scheduled meeting strengthens what
      it's already doing: join links, event context.
    image: /shots/grod/agenda.webp
    alt: >-
      GRØD's agenda, with the next meeting docked at the top thirty minutes
      ahead of time and a record button waiting to be pressed, above the week's
      earlier meetings.
---
I made GRØD because I thought it was absurd that you should pay monthly for meeting notes. Absurd that one would want a bot to join your call. Absurd that nothing actually felt like a Maac app. Not quite made from anger, but definitely something with a lot of big feelings about how these kind of apps should really work.
